  The repo has no test setup and no linter yet. Here's the plan for testing this change:

  - [1mUnit tests for [38;5;153msession.js[0m use a small fake [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m object. They cover:
    - saving a user and reading it back
    - [38;5;153mgetCurrentUserId()[39m and [38;5;153mclearSession()[39m
    - [38;5;153msetCurrentUser[39m throwing when the [38;5;153muserId[39m is missing
    - a corrupt stored value getting cleared and returning [38;5;153mnull[39m
    - storage that throws errors producing a warning and [38;5;153mnull[39m, without crashing
  - [1mUnit tests for [38;5;153mlogin()[0m check that it returns a [38;5;153muserId[39m. Two small tweaks are needed so tests can load the code
    outside a browser:
    - [38;5;153msession.js[39m and [38;5;153mapp.js[39m also export their functions when loaded by Node.
    - [38;5;153mapp.js[39m only wires up the form when [38;5;153mdocument[39m exists.

    Neither tweak changes how they behave in the browser.
  - [1mThe submit handler[0m gets a short manual check: open [38;5;153mindex.html[39m, log in, confirm [38;5;153mcurrentUser[39m appears in DevTools under
    Application → Session Storage, and confirm a reload keeps it.

  [1mWhich tools to set up?[0m It's cheapest to add them now, while there's almost no code. I recommend Node's built-in test
  runner ([38;5;153mnode --test[39m). It needs no new packages and covers everything above.
